Z||000000||mikemrq||06-02-2001||07:36 PM||||on my '86 fiero, v6, 4sp the coil under my dist. cap (i believe its called a pick up coil) looks to be all rusty, is that normal? if it should be replaced, must the dist. be taken out first?||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||06-02-2001||07:50 PM||||Yes if it appears corroded and connecter to ignition module corroded as well they both should be replaced.  You do have to remove the dist to replace pick up coil.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadaver||06-03-2001||02:53 PM||||It takes like 1/2 hour and $12.||||reg||1
Z||000003||beer_stud_21||06-03-2001||04:01 PM||||Just because the pickup coil looks bad doesn't mean it IS bad.  It can be tested with a simple multimeter.  The instructions are in the haynes manual.  To quote the manual p149-150: <P> "Connect an ohmmeter to each termain of the pick-up coil lead and ground  Flex the leads by hand to check for intermittent opens.  The ohmmeter should indicate infinite at all times.  If it doesn't , the pickup coil is defective.  <BR>Connect the ohmmeter to both terminals of the pickup coil lead.  Flex the leads by hand to check for intermittent opens.  The ohmmeter should read one steady value between 500-1500 ohms as the leads are flexed by hand.  If it doesn't, the pickup coil is defective. "<P>If it is defective you can get a new one for like 10 bucks at Napa etc.<P>-Marlin<BR>85GT/auto *Now Running*||||reg||1
